@startuml r19-item-state-machine
title rb-object-item State Machine\nListener Registration Lifecycle

state "Created" as CR : createElement('rb-object-item')
state "Plain HTMLElement" as PLAIN : define() not yet run\nNo prototype methods\nsetAttribute = DOM-only
state "Upgraded (disconnected)" as UPG : RbObjectItem prototype\n_connected=false\nNo listeners registered
state "Connected (initialized)" as CONN : connectedCallback ran\n.object-item class ✓\nclick listener ✓\nrender() ran ✓\n_connected=true
state "Broken" as BROKEN #pink : appendChild'd as plain HTMLElement\nNO connectedCallback ever\nNO listeners\nNO .object-item class\nICON ONLY / DEAD
state "Disconnected (cleanup)" as DISC : disconnectedCallback ran\nlistener removed\nunsub ViewBus
state "Re-connected" as RECONN : connectedCallback runs AGAIN\nlistener added AGAIN\n⚠️ DOUBLE if not guarded

CR --> PLAIN : define() not yet run
CR --> UPG : define() already ran

PLAIN --> BROKEN : appendChild()\n(no connectedCallback\non plain HTMLElement)
PLAIN --> UPG : define() runs later\n(browser upgrades\nin-DOM elements)
note right of PLAIN : KEY: if element was\nalready appendChild'd\nBEFORE define() runs,\nbrowser upgrades it\nAND fires connectedCallback

UPG --> CONN : appendChild()\n→ connectedCallback()
CONN --> DISC : removeChild() / innerHTML=''\n→ disconnectedCallback()
DISC --> RECONN : appendChild() again\n(same element re-used)

BROKEN -[#red]-> UPG : define() runs\n→ browser upgrades\n→ connectedCallback fires\n(element already in DOM)
note left of BROKEN #yellow : WAIT — if define() runs\nwhile element is in DOM,\nbrowser DOES fire\nconnectedCallback!\nSo BROKEN→CONN should happen.\nWhy doesn't it?

note bottom of BROKEN #red
  ANSWER: innerHTML='' in renderItems()
  DESTROYS the element BEFORE
  define() runs. Sequence:
  1. FILE_ADDED → renderItems() → innerHTML=''
  2. createElement (plain) → setAttribute → appendChild
  3. NEXT FILE_ADDED → renderItems() → innerHTML=''
     DESTROYS the element from step 2
  4. repeat for each FILE_ADDED
  5. define() finally runs
  6. Only the LAST batch's elements
     are still in DOM to be upgraded

  The intermediate items are created
  AND DESTROYED before define() runs.
  They never get a chance to upgrade.
end note

@enduml
